---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const article = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: 'Pakistan Passport Renewal — Complete 2026 Guide',
  author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'VisaPath.pk' },
  publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'VisaPath.pk' },
  datePublished: '2026-10-24',
};

const faq = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: [
    { '@type': 'Question', name: 'How long does Pakistani passport renewal take?',
      acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'Normal renewal takes 10 working days. Urgent service delivers within 7 working days, and fast-track within 24 hours (fees increase accordingly).' } },
    { '@type': 'Question', name: 'Can I renew my Pakistani passport before it expires?',
      acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'Yes. You can renew up to 1 year before expiry at any DGIP office with no penalty. Remaining validity is not carried forward — you get a fresh 5 or 10 year passport.' } },
    { '@type': 'Question', name: 'What is the e-Passport?',
      acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'Pakistan e-Passport is a biometric passport with embedded chip. It is being rolled out across major cities and provides faster immigration clearance abroad.' } },
  ],
};
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Pakistan Passport Renewal — Complete 2026 Guide"
  description="Step-by-step guide to renewing your Pakistani passport (MRP and e-Passport). Fees, documents, DGIP offices, overseas renewal, urgent service."
  schema={[article, faq]}
>
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <nav class="text-sm text-gray-500 mb-4">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-brand-600">Home</a> ›
      <a href="/guides/" class="hover:text-brand-600">Guides</a> ›
      <span>Pakistan Passport Renewal</span>
    </nav>

    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-4">Pakistan Passport Renewal — Complete 2026 Guide</h1>
    <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm mb-8">Last updated: October 2026 · 10 minute read</p>

    <section class="prose max-w-none">
      <p class="lead text-lg">
        Your Pakistani passport is required for all international travel. This guide covers everything about renewing it in 2026 — from DGIP offices in Pakistan to Pakistani missions abroad, standard and urgent service, and the new e-Passport.
      </p>

      <h2>When to Renew</h2>
      <p>
        Renew your passport:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Within 1 year of expiry if you travel internationally</li>
        <li>If you have less than 6 months validity (many countries require 6+ months)</li>
        <li>When you run out of blank pages</li>
        <li>If your name, address, or other details change</li>
        <li>Immediately if lost, stolen, or damaged</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        You can renew up to 1 year before expiry with <strong>no penalty</strong>. Remaining validity is not transferred — you get a fresh 5 or 10 year passport from the issue date.
      </p>

      <h2>Types of Pakistani Passports</h2>
      <h3>Machine Readable Passport (MRP)</h3>
      <p>
        Standard Pakistani passport since 2004. Available in 36-page and 72-page options, with 5-year or 10-year validity. Suitable for most travellers.
      </p>

      <h3>e-Passport (Biometric)</h3>
      <p>
        Rolled out progressively from 2024. Contains an embedded chip with biometric data for faster international immigration clearance. Available in major DGIP centres; being extended nationwide. Slightly higher fee but preferred for frequent international travellers.
      </p>

      <h2>Passport Fees (2026)</h2>
      <h3>MRP Standard (10 Working Days)</h3>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Pages</th><th>5 Years</th><th>10 Years</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>36 pages</td><td>PKR 4,500</td><td>PKR 9,000</td></tr>
          <tr><td>72 pages</td><td>PKR 6,500</td><td>PKR 13,500</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h3>Urgent (7 Working Days)</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>36-page 5-year: PKR 7,500</li>
        <li>36-page 10-year: PKR 15,000</li>
        <li>72-page 5-year: PKR 10,500</li>
        <li>72-page 10-year: PKR 20,000</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Fast Track (24 Hours)</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>36-page 5-year: PKR 15,000</li>
        <li>36-page 10-year: PKR 30,000</li>
        <li>72-page 5-year: PKR 20,000</li>
        <li>72-page 10-year: PKR 40,000</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>e-Passport (additional)</h3>
      <p>
        e-Passport typically costs PKR 2,000–3,000 more than equivalent MRP for the same validity and pages.
      </p>

      <h2>Documents Needed</h2>
      <h3>For Adult Renewal</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Current CNIC (original + copy)</li>
        <li>Previous passport (original + copy of data page)</li>
        <li>Appointment confirmation (if applied online)</li>
        <li>Fee payment slip from bank</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>For Minor Children (under 18)</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>B-Form or Juvenile ID</li>
        <li>Both parents' CNICs</li>
        <li>Both parents must be present</li>
        <li>Previous passport (if being renewed)</li>
        <li>Birth certificate (if first passport)</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>For First-Time Applicants (Adults)</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>CNIC</li>
        <li>CNIC of father/husband/mother for verification</li>
        <li>Educational certificates (if required)</li>
        <li>No previous passport required but easier if previously issued</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Where to Apply in Pakistan</h2>
      <h3>DGIP Offices</h3>
      <p>
        Directorate General of Immigration & Passports (DGIP) offices are located in all major cities. Main centres:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Islamabad: Awami Markaz G-9/1, Blue Area</li>
        <li>Lahore: Shadman, Garden Town, Johar Town, DHA</li>
        <li>Karachi: Civil Lines, Saddar, Clifton, Gulistan-e-Johar</li>
        <li>Rawalpindi, Faisalabad, Multan, Peshawar, Quetta — regional offices</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Online Appointment</h3>
      <p>
        You can book appointments and start applications at <code>onlinemrp.dgip.gov.pk</code>. This saves queue time at the office. Walk-in is still available but slower.
      </p>

      <h2>Process at the Office</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Arrive at your appointment time with documents</li>
        <li>Token issued at reception</li>
        <li>Data verification counter — officer checks your details</li>
        <li>Biometrics capture — fingerprints and photo</li>
        <li>Signature capture</li>
        <li>Fee receipt verification</li>
        <li>Acknowledgment slip with tracking number issued</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Total time at office: 30 minutes to 2 hours depending on queue.
      </p>

      <h2>Collection</h2>
      <p>
        Options:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>In-person collection</strong> on the stated date — bring acknowledgment slip and CNIC</li>
        <li><strong>Courier delivery</strong> to home address (small additional fee) — selected at application</li>
        <li><strong>Nominated person</strong> can collect with authority letter</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Overseas Pakistanis — Renewing Abroad</h2>
      <p>
        Pakistani embassies and consulates abroad issue new passports to overseas Pakistanis. Major missions:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>London, Manchester, Bradford (UK)</li>
        <li>New York, Washington, Houston, Los Angeles (US)</li>
        <li>Toronto (Canada)</li>
        <li>Dubai, Abu Dhabi (UAE)</li>
        <li>Riyadh, Jeddah (Saudi Arabia)</li>
        <li>Many other embassies worldwide</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Process Abroad</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>Book appointment at the mission (website varies)</li>
        <li>Attend with current passport, CNIC/NICOP, fee</li>
        <li>Biometrics captured</li>
        <li>Processing time: typically 4–8 weeks (longer than Pakistan)</li>
        <li>Collect or receive by courier</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        <strong>NICOP (National ID Card for Overseas Pakistanis)</strong> is often required. If you don't have one, apply for it first through NADRA.
      </p>

      <h2>Lost or Stolen Passports</h2>
      <p>
        If your passport is lost or stolen:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>File a police report (FIR) immediately</li>
        <li>If abroad, report to nearest Pakistani mission</li>
        <li>Apply for a replacement passport (treated as new application)</li>
        <li>Expect additional scrutiny — repeated losses can trigger investigation</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Common Issues</h2>
      <h3>Name Mismatches</h3>
      <p>
        Your passport name must match your CNIC exactly. If CNIC and passport names differ, you may need to update CNIC first (via NADRA) before renewing passport.
      </p>

      <h3>Clearance Required</h3>
      <p>
        Certain cases require Ministry of Interior clearance (specific professions, previous legal issues, name changes). This adds 4–12 weeks to processing.
      </p>

      <h3>Expired Passport Used for Visa Application</h3>
      <p>
        If a visa (UK, US, Schengen) is stamped in a passport that then expires or you renew, the visa remains valid. Travel with both old (with visa) and new passport.
      </p>

      <h2>Related Resources</h2>
      <p>
        <a href="/tools/passport-validity-checker/">Passport Validity Checker</a> ·
        <a href="/tools/visa-processing-time/">Visa Processing Times</a>
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
